## Summary

Implement the XOR masking algorithm for WebSocket payloads per RFC 6455 Section 5.3.

## Specification Reference

RFC 6455 Section 5.3 - Client-to-Server Masking:

> The masking key is a 32-bit value chosen at random by the client.
> ...
> Octet i of the transformed data ("transformed-octet-i") is the XOR of
> octet i of the original data ("original-octet-i") with octet at index
> i modulo 4 of the masking key ("masking-key-octet-j"):
>
> j = i MOD 4
> transformed-octet-i = original-octet-i XOR masking-key-octet-j

Key points:
- Client MUST mask all frames sent to server
- Server MUST NOT mask frames sent to client
- Same algorithm for masking and unmasking (XOR is self-inverse)
- Must not leak timing information based on payload content

## Implementation Details

### File: src/websocket/protocol/masking.zig

```zig
const std = @import("std");

/// Apply or remove masking from WebSocket payload data.
/// This function works in-place and can be used for both masking and unmasking
/// since XOR is self-inverse.
///
/// Per RFC 6455 Section 5.3:
/// transformed-octet-i = original-octet-i XOR masking-key-octet-(i MOD 4)
pub fn applyMask(data: []u8, masking_key: [4]u8) void {
    // Optimize for common case: process 4 bytes at a time
    const key_u32 = std.mem.readInt(u32, &masking_key, .little);

    var i: usize = 0;

    // Process 4 bytes at a time using u32 XOR
    while (i + 4 <= data.len) : (i += 4) {
        const chunk = std.mem.readInt(u32, data[i..][0..4], .little);
        std.mem.writeInt(u32, data[i..][0..4], chunk ^ key_u32, .little);
    }

    // Process remaining bytes
    while (i < data.len) : (i += 1) {
        data[i] ^= masking_key[i % 4];
    }
}

/// Apply masking with offset (for streaming/chunked processing)
/// Useful when processing payload in multiple chunks
pub fn applyMaskWithOffset(data: []u8, masking_key: [4]u8, offset: usize) void {
    for (data, 0..) |*byte, i| {
        byte.* ^= masking_key[(offset + i) % 4];
    }
}

/// Generate a cryptographically secure masking key
pub fn generateMaskingKey() [4]u8 {
    var key: [4]u8 = undefined;
    std.crypto.random.bytes(&key);
    return key;
}

/// Mask data into a new buffer (non-destructive)
pub fn maskToBuffer(dest: []u8, src: []const u8, masking_key: [4]u8) void {
    std.debug.assert(dest.len >= src.len);
    for (src, 0..) |byte, i| {
        dest[i] = byte ^ masking_key[i % 4];
    }
}
```

## SIMD Optimization (Optional Enhancement)

For large payloads, SIMD can significantly speed up masking:

```zig
/// SIMD-optimized masking for large payloads
pub fn applyMaskSimd(data: []u8, masking_key: [4]u8) void {
    const Vector = @Vector(16, u8);

    // Expand 4-byte key to 16-byte vector (repeat 4 times)
    var key_vec: Vector = undefined;
    inline for (0..4) |i| {
        inline for (0..4) |j| {
            key_vec[i * 4 + j] = masking_key[j];
        }
    }

    var i: usize = 0;

    // Process 16 bytes at a time with SIMD
    while (i + 16 <= data.len) : (i += 16) {
        const chunk: *align(1) Vector = @ptrCast(data[i..][0..16]);
        chunk.* = chunk.* ^ key_vec;
    }

    // Fallback for remaining bytes
    while (i < data.len) : (i += 1) {
        data[i] ^= masking_key[i % 4];
    }
}
```

