## Summary

Define all WebSocket opcodes, frame types, and related constants per RFC 6455 Section 5.2.

## Specification Reference

RFC 6455 Section 5.2 - Base Framing Protocol:
- Opcodes are 4-bit values
- Control frames: 0x8 (close), 0x9 (ping), 0xA (pong)
- Data frames: 0x0 (continuation), 0x1 (text), 0x2 (binary)
- Reserved: 0x3-0x7 (non-control), 0xB-0xF (control)

## Implementation Details

### File: src/websocket/protocol/opcodes.zig

```zig
/// WebSocket opcodes per RFC 6455 Section 5.2
pub const Opcode = enum(u4) {
    /// Continuation frame (fragmentation)
    continuation = 0x0,

    /// Text frame (UTF-8 encoded)
    text = 0x1,

    /// Binary frame
    binary = 0x2,

    // 0x3-0x7 reserved for non-control frames

    /// Connection close
    close = 0x8,

    /// Ping (keep-alive request)
    ping = 0x9,

    /// Pong (keep-alive response)
    pong = 0xA,

    // 0xB-0xF reserved for control frames
    _,

    /// Check if opcode is for a control frame (>= 0x8)
    pub fn isControl(self: Opcode) bool {
        return @intFromEnum(self) >= 0x8;
    }

    /// Check if opcode is for a data frame (< 0x8)
    pub fn isData(self: Opcode) bool {
        return @intFromEnum(self) < 0x8;
    }

    /// Check if opcode is reserved/unknown
    pub fn isReserved(self: Opcode) bool {
        const val = @intFromEnum(self);
        return (val >= 0x3 and val <= 0x7) or val >= 0xB;
    }
};

/// Frame type classification
pub const FrameType = enum {
    /// Continuation of a fragmented message
    continuation,
    /// Complete text message or first fragment
    text,
    /// Complete binary message or first fragment  
    binary,
    /// Close control frame
    close,
    /// Ping control frame
    ping,
    /// Pong control frame
    pong,
    /// Unknown/reserved opcode
    unknown,
};
```

